<h2>Two instruments, two characters</h2>
<p>Order VIII Rule 6 permits a defendant in a suit for recovery of money to claim a <strong>set-off</strong>: an ascertained sum of money, legally recoverable from the plaintiff, where both parties fill the same character as they fill in the suit, and within the pecuniary limits of the court. The written statement pleading set-off has the effect of a plaint in a cross-suit, but the set-off itself works as a defence — it extinguishes or reduces the plaintiff&rsquo;s claim.</p>
<p>Alongside the codified &ldquo;legal&rdquo; set-off, courts recognise an <strong>equitable set-off</strong> for claims that are not ascertained sums but arise out of the same transaction or a connected series of transactions — the classic example being a claim for damages for defective supply raised against a claim for the price of that very supply. Equitable set-off is discretionary, not demandable as of right, and the connection between the cross-demands is its foundation.</p>
<p>The <strong>counterclaim</strong> under Rule 6A is a different creature altogether: a cross-suit prosecuted inside the plaintiff&rsquo;s suit. It need not be limited to money, need not arise from the same transaction, and can exceed the plaintiff&rsquo;s claim — subject to the pecuniary jurisdiction of the court.</p>

<div class="compare">
  <div class="col old"><strong>Set-off (Rule 6)</strong><br>Ascertained sum; same character; a shield that reduces or extinguishes the claim; falls with the suit.</div>
  <div class="arrow">↔</div>
  <div class="col new"><strong>Counterclaim (Rule 6A)</strong><br>Any cause of action against the plaintiff; a sword that can end in an independent decree; survives withdrawal or dismissal of the suit.</div>
</div>

<h2>The timing discipline</h2>
<p>Rule 6A fixes the window: the cause of action for the counterclaim may accrue <em>before or after the filing of the suit</em>, but it must accrue <strong>before the defendant has delivered the defence or before the time limited for delivering it has expired</strong>. In commercial suits this dovetails with the strict written-statement regime — the defence must come within thirty days, with any extension capped at one hundred and twenty days from service of summons, after which the right to file stands forfeited. A defendant who sits on its cross-claims through that window will struggle to introduce them later, and courts scrutinise belated counterclaims for prejudice to the plaintiff and for attempts to derail a trial already framed.</p>

<div class="flow">
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Summons served.</strong> The clock for the written statement — and effectively for the counterclaim — begins to run.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Written statement with counterclaim.</strong> The counterclaim is specifically pleaded, separately valued, supported by court fees, and verified with a Statement of Truth under Order VI Rule 15A as applicable to commercial disputes.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>Plaintiff answers.</strong> The counterclaim is treated as a plaint; the plaintiff files a written statement to it, and the rules governing written statements apply to that reply. Default exposes the plaintiff to the counterclaim being decided against it.</div>
  <div class="fstep"><strong>One trial, two claims.</strong> Issues are framed on both, evidence is led once, and the court may pass judgment for the balance found due either way.</div>
</div>

<h2>The commercial-court overlay</h2>
<p>In a suit governed by the Commercial Courts Act, 2015, the counterclaim lives under the same discipline as the plaint it answers. Pleadings must carry the Statement of Truth; admission and denial of documents must be specific; costs follow conduct; and the counterclaim, like the claim, is exposed to <strong>summary judgment</strong> — either side may contend that the other&rsquo;s claim has no real prospect of success. A counterclaim inserted only to complicate a clean invoice claim can therefore be disposed of summarily, with costs; equally, a genuine counterclaim can defeat the plaintiff&rsquo;s own summary-judgment application by showing the dispute is triable.</p>

<h2>Counterclaim or separate suit?</h2>
<div class="tiles">
  <div class="tile"><strong>Forum and value.</strong> The counterclaim cannot exceed the court&rsquo;s pecuniary limits, and its own specified value matters in commercial litigation. A cross-claim that belongs in a different forum is better sued separately.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Mediation gate.</strong> A fresh commercial suit without urgent interim relief must pass through pre-institution mediation under Section 12A; a counterclaim in a pending suit does not — a real tactical difference.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Limitation.</strong> Filing a counterclaim does not rewind time. Limitation for the cross-claim is tested on its own terms, so a defendant waiting for the plaintiff to sue first gambles with its own claim.</div>
  <div class="tile"><strong>Exclusion risk.</strong> Under Rule 6C the plaintiff may apply to have the counterclaim excluded and tried as an independent suit where joint trial is inconvenient — a factor when the cross-claim is factually remote.</div>
</div>

<h2>A drafting checklist for the defendant</h2>
<div class="check">
  <p>Plead the counterclaim specifically and separately from the defence, with its own cause-of-action paragraphs, valuation and relief clause.</p>
  <p>Pay ad valorem court fees on the counterclaim as on a plaint; annex the documents relied on and disclose them as the commercial-court rules require.</p>
  <p>Verify with a Statement of Truth; an evasive or false verification carries consequences in costs and credibility.</p>
  <p>Where the cross-demand is an ascertained sum from the same dealings, plead set-off in the alternative, so the shield remains even if the sword fails.</p>
  <p>Diarise limitation for the cross-claim independently of the suit — and move early, within the written-statement window.</p>
</div>
<div class="note">For plaintiffs, the lesson is symmetrical: a recovery suit should anticipate the probable counterclaim, address the debit notes and quality correspondence in the plaint itself, and answer any counterclaim within time — Rule 6E lets the court pronounce judgment against a plaintiff who defaults in replying to a counterclaim.</div>
